orbitop for Debian: the counter release
=======================================

The switch "Release counters" in orbitop's About dialog lets every account on this
computer read the GPU and energy counters, after the admin password. The setting
for the whole machine is the empty marker file /etc/orbitop/counters-released.
Only "/usr/lib/orbitop/counterhelper on" creates it; no package script ever does.

Deviation from Debian Policy 10.7.3
-----------------------------------

Debian Policy 10.7.3 says that "configuration files must be preserved when the
package is removed, and only deleted when the package is purged". The marker is
deleted on "remove" already: prerm runs "counterhelper off", which deletes it and
then takes the capability and the read bits back, and postrm deletes it once more.
A marker kept after "apt remove orbitop" would release the counters again on the
next "apt install orbitop", for every account and without asking the admin.
"apt purge orbitop" also removes the empty directory /etc/orbitop.

Triggers
--------

A new copy of /usr/bin/intel_gpu_top (installed, reinstalled or updated) has no
file capability. postinst therefore runs "counterhelper sync" on "configure" and
when dpkg triggers it for that path (orbitop.triggers); "sync" changes nothing
without the marker. That orbitop sets a capability on a file of another package
is the deliberate exception to the dpkg guideline that a file trigger "should not
generally modify that file again".

If the helper cannot run on removal, the marker still goes, but the capability
stays on /usr/bin/intel_gpu_top, and the energy counters stay readable until the
next restart. Then run "sudo setcap -r /usr/bin/intel_gpu_top" and restart.
